use console.println

/// Rewrites `old` as `new` in `text`, where `old` is whatever its caller chose.
/// Not the standard library: an ordinary Cove function with a caller, between
/// `main` and the operation that refuses, so that the diagnostic below has a
/// frame to blame that is neither the entry point nor the machine.
fn rewritten(text: String, old: String, new: String) -> String {
  text.replace(old, new)
}

/// Pins `String.replace`'s refusal of an empty `old`, word for word.
///
/// `String.split`'s refusal and this one share a rule and share nothing else:
/// the message names a different method and a different parameter, and **the
/// help is not the same sentence**. `split` is pointed at `chars()`, which is
/// the operation that means what an empty separator would have meant; `replace`
/// is told that `old` is the text to look for and that an empty one names none,
/// because there is no operation for it to be pointed at. A brief that assumed
/// the two refusals were one refusal would have changed this line.
///
/// This is the half of `String.replace`'s contract that `values_string_replace`
/// cannot hold, because a refusal stops the run and takes every row after it:
/// one case, one refusal. [ADR 0064](../../../docs/adr/0064-an-intrinsic-names-a-machine-not-a-method.md)'s
/// Decision 8 gates a migration on the diagnostic being unchanged, and this
/// file is where "unchanged" is written down. Issue
/// [#461](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/461) is why that gate is not met
/// yet: a Cove body has nothing to raise with, and nothing that would carry the
/// `rule` and the `help` if it had.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  let out = rewritten("a,b,c", "", ";")
  println("{out}")?
  Ok(())
}
